#### Simulation results: \(\Lambda_{\rm C}\) reconstruction

The simulated data sample for \(\Lambda_{\rm C}\) reconstruction was 19.5k central HIJING events. Half of it had 5 \(\Lambda_{\rm C}\) added per event, the other half had 30 \(\Lambda_{\rm C}\) added per event, all with 100% branching ratio to \(\rm K^{-}+\pi^{+}+p\) and flat \(p_{\rm T}\) distribution. The pile-up in PIXEL detector at level of RHIC-II luminosity was simulated. The yield of \(\Lambda_{\rm C}\) in central collisions was estimated to be either 20% of \(\rm D^{0}\) yield at all \(p_{\rm T}\) or the enhancement of \(\Lambda_{\rm C}\)/\(\rm D^{0}\) ratio was considered of the same magnitude as the \(\Lambda\)/\(\rm K^{0}_{S}\) enhancement observed in 200 GeV Au+Au colisions by STAR [111]. The yield of \(\Lambda_{\rm C}\) in peripheral (60-80%) collisions was taken to be 20% of \(\rm D^{0}\) yield at the same \(p_{\rm T}\). The results presented here are based on [124] with improvement in topological cut optimization.

The same set of cut variables was used to select the \(\Lambda_{\rm C}\) candidates as the one used for \(\rm D^{0}\), except the cut \(DCA_{V0}\) was rescaled by track pointing resolution 5. The invariant mass cut was fixed at \(2.27<M_{inv}<2.31\) GeV/\(c^{2}\). The other three cuts were automatically tuned (3-dimensional scanning of the parameter space) to achieve the highest signal significance. This was performed separately for 3 \(p_{\rm T}\) bins (\(2-3,3-4,4-5\) GeV/\(c\)), three different signal and background assumptions (central collisions, central collisions with enhancement, peripheral collisions) and two different PID options (optional requirement of all three tracks being below TOF PID separation thresholds: GoodPID, or allowing for contamination by other particle species: RealPID).

