#### Comparison to results from PHENIX experiment

Recently the PHENIX Collaboration presented measurement of jet \(R_{\rm CP}\) from 200 GeV d+Au collisions [144, 145]. The details of this analysis are briefly described, followed by the results. Finally a comparison to the \(R_{\rm CP}\) measurement in this thesis is presented.

The PHENIX \(R_{\rm CP}\) results were obtained with \(\rm anti-k_{T}\) jet algorithm with resolution parameter \(R=0.3\) and \(R=0.5\). The PHENIX analysis uses different lower \(p_{\rm T}\) cut on tracks and towers: \(p_{\rm T,cut}=0.4\) GeV/\(c\) (as opposed to \(p_{\rm T,cut}=0.2\) GeV/\(c\) used in STAR). PHENIX Collaboration uses jet embedding into minimum bias events and unfolding as their only correction, i.e. without event-wise background determination and subtraction. The event sample used is not minimum bias but there is a trigger requiring a high-\(p_{\rm T}\) electron/photon with \(p_{\rm T}>1.6\) GeV/\(c\). Also the centrality selection for the peripheral bin is different: 60-88% as opposed to 40-100% used in STAR.

The nuclear modification factor \(R_{\rm CP}\) by the PHENIX Collaboration is shown in Figure 6.11 for \(R=0.3\) and in Figure 6.12 for \(R=0.5\). What is observed is centrality-dependent _suppression_. The jet suppression for \(R=0.3\) is almost constant with jet \(p_{\rm T}\) and has a value of \(R_{\rm CP}\approx 0.75\pm 0.1\). The suppression is smaller for higher value of resolution parameter \(R\). In this jet \(p_{\rm T}\) region there may

