The analysis of \(R_{\rm CP}\) at STAR data with bin-by-bin correction was run with \(p_{\rm T,cut}=0.4\) GeV/\(c\) and \(R=0.3\), \(R=0.5\) to compare to the PHENIX results. It was previously shown that the bin-by-bin correction is consistent with the \(\Delta p_{\rm T}\) correction, so only the former was used for this comparison. The \(R_{\rm CP}\) results for comparison with PHENIX are shown in Figure 6.13 (\(R=0.3\)) and Figure 6.14 (\(R=0.5\)).

The \(p_{\rm T}\) integrated values of \(R_{\rm CP}\) in intervals \(9.3<p_{\rm T}<14.1\) GeV/\(c\), \(p_{\rm T}>14.1\) GeV/\(c\) with statistical errors are shown in the figures, the systematic uncertainties are \(\approx 10\%\). The \(R_{\rm CP}\) for \(R=0.3\) is systematically above unity by \(\approx 10\%\), i.e. at the edge of systematic uncertainty. For \(R=0.5\) an enhancement stronger than for the standard STAR cuts (\(R=0.4\), \(p_{\rm T,cut}=0.2\) GeV/\(c\)) is observed over the whole \(p_{\rm T}\) range. In the \(p_{\rm T}\) range of PHENIX measurement the STAR and PHENIX results are not consistent.

To avoid the possible effect of different centrality selection, comparison of jet \(p_{\rm T}\) spectra in the common (0-20%, 20-40%) centrality bins should be done. This is however not possible at the moment, since the PHENIX Collaboration has not presented the fully corrected jet \(p_{\rm T}\) spectra yet, only their \(R_{\rm CP}\) ratios.

